.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt DEADMAN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm deadman
.Nd send mail when jobs stop checking in
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm deadman
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Cm check | status | token
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a dead man's switch.
Each job it watches, called a check, pings a URL of its own whenever it
runs, through
.Xr deadman.cgi 8 ;
when the pings stop,
.Nm
sends mail.
It is meant to run every minute from
.Xr cron 8 .
.Pp
Each check, listed in
.Xr deadman.conf 5 ,
has a period, how often it should ping, and a grace time, how much longer
to wait before giving up on it.
A check is in one of four states:
.Bl -tag -width "late"
.It new
It has never pinged.
.It up
Its last ping came within its period.
.It late
Its period has passed, but not its grace time.
.It down
Its period and grace time have passed without a ping; or its last ping
reported a failure; or it reported starting, and did not finish within
its grace time.
.El
.Pp
A job can ping in three ways.
A plain ping says that it ran.
A ping to
.Pa start
says that it has started, so that a run that never finishes counts as
down once the grace time has passed; it does not otherwise change the
check's state.
A ping to
.Pa fail
says that it ran and failed, and puts the check down at once.
.Pp
.Nm
sends mail to a check's address when it goes down, and again when it is
up again.
Being late sends nothing.
Mail that cannot be sent is tried again on the next run.
.Pp
.Nm
reads its configuration as root, then becomes the
.Dq _deadman
user, and
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
confine it to its two directories and
.Xr sendmail 8 .
On each run it makes the ping files for any new check, and removes those
of any check no longer configured, whose ping URL then stops working.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm check
Work out each check's state, and send mail about any that has changed.
This is the default.
.It Cm status
Show each check's state, its last ping, and when the next is due.
.It Cm token
Print a new token, 32 random hexadecimal digits, for a check's ping URL.
It needs neither root nor the configuration.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar file
Read the configuration from
.Ar file
instead of
.Pa /etc/deadman.conf .
.It Fl n
Show the mail that
.Cm check
would send, and change nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/www/cgi-bin/deadman.cgi" -compact
.It Pa /etc/deadman.conf
The checks.
.It Pa /var/www/deadman/
The ping files, three for each check, named after its token; their
modification times say when each kind of ping last came.
.It Pa /var/db/deadman/
Each check's state when it last changed, and when.
.It Pa /var/www/cgi-bin/deadman.cgi
What the ping URLs run.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Mail that could not be sent is an error, which
.Xr cron 8
reports by mail to root.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make a token, then add a check that should ping daily, with a day's
grace:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ deadman token
6b1e0c3f9a7d42e8b5c1d0f4a2e9c7b3
# echo "backups 6b1e0c3f9a7d42e8b5c1d0f4a2e9c7b3 1d 1d root" \e
	>>/etc/deadman.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Run it every minute, from root's
.Xr crontab 5 ,
only once at a time, and with no mail unless it fails:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
*	*	*	*	*	-sn /usr/local/sbin/deadman
.Ed
.Pp
Ping from the end of a job, using
.Xr ftp 1
on
.Ox
or
.Xr curl 1
elsewhere:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ftp -o /dev/null -V https://deadman.example.org/ping/6b1e0c3f9a7d42e8b5c1d0f4a2e9c7b3
curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 -o /dev/null https://deadman.example.org/ping/6b1e...
.Ed
.Pp
See where things stand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# deadman status
NAME     STATE  LAST PING              DUE
backups  up     2026-11-16 03:17 CET   2026-11-17 03:17 CET
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crontab 5 ,
.Xr deadman.conf 5 ,
.Xr cron 8 ,
.Xr deadman.cgi 8 ,
.Xr sendmail 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joe Abley Aq Mt jabley@strandkip.nl ,
with Claude.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
notices a change only when it runs, so a check goes down up to a
minute after its time, with the crontab line above.
.Pp
Nothing watches
.Nm
itself.
If it, or the machine it runs on, stops, no mail comes at all: run it
somewhere other than what it watches.
